@using Timesheet.Mvc.RazorComponents.Components
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Timesheet";
}

<section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">C# .NET &middot; MVC hosting Blazor components &middot; Azure</p>
        <h1>Timesheet: hybrid MVC and Razor Components</h1>
        <p class="lead">An MVC page (.cshtml) hosting an interactive Blazor component (.razor) that adds timesheet entries.
            One of three front ends on the same application layer; this one was an experiment that was abandoned.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<main class="wrap grid">
    <component type="typeof(TimesheetCrud)" render-mode="ServerPrerendered" />

    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="abandoned-title">
        <h2 id="abandoned-title">Why it was abandoned</h2>
        <p>Razor Components in .NET 8 are designed for Blazor-first apps, not MVC. The hybrid story is "supported", but in practice:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>the pipeline order is fragile;</li>
            <li>hydration fails silently;</li>
            <li>errors hide behind <code>_framework/blazor.server.js</code>;</li>
            <li>namespaces, routing order and antiforgery all matter;</li>
            <li>the hub must be mapped in the right place, and the root component must exist;</li>
            <li>the layout must not interfere, and MVC tag helpers can break hydration.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Too brittle, so the next step went straight to Blazor (see the Blazor Server version).</p>
    </section>

    <partial name="_Variants" model="@("Hybrid")" />
</main>
